<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Chariot: rendezvous and mailbox for Pegasus. Not a peer - it never holds
       a note, never merges an update, and cannot read a word either party
       writes. docs/Chariot_Design.md is the record; §4 is why "relay" and
       "peer" are different things here.

       NO WEB STACK, and that is the whole point of speaking the Pegasus frame
       protocol. No ASP.NET Core, no SignalR, no Entity Framework, no OpenAPI.
       The forked C# server had all four and this has a TCP listener. §3
       records what that decision bought and what it deferred. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>EmuSen.Chariot</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>EmuSen.Chariot</RootNamespace>

    <!-- The relay's own version, on a `v*` tag. Not the same number as the
         EmuSen.Pegasus.Core package it speaks through, which is at 0.3.0 —
         that one versions on the protocol, this one on the server. Pegasus the
         desktop application is at 0.3.0 as well, and that stays a coincidence of
         the two being released together rather than a rule that they move
         together. What has to agree between the two programs is the protocol
         number in Hello, not the version on the download.

         0.2.0 was spent rather than skipped: it was tagged, it built three
         platforms, and it published nothing, because the matrix named a runner
         image that had been retired - §12.3.

         THE RELEASED VERSION COMES FROM THE TAG: release.yml strips the `v` and
         passes -p:Version, so the tag decides what the binary reports and what
         the archives are called. This number is the default for a local
         `dotnet publish` and is kept in step so the two never disagree - §12.

         0.4.0 is messaging: a durable message queue, acknowledgements, and a
         card directory. It is a bump a relay operator cannot skip, because the
         protocol moved with it — a 0.3.0 client cannot sign in here, and Hello
         says so in the first frame rather than letting it be found further
         down. §13 and §14.

         0.4.1 is the queue bound 0.4.0 claimed and did not have: the conditional
         insert meant to enforce it read `WHERE 1 = 1`, so the message queue took
         post for an absent recipient without limit and no sender was ever told a
         message had not landed. The protocol did NOT move - Hello still says 5,
         and a 0.4.0 client signs in here unchanged - but an operator should take
         it, because the version it fixes fills a disk and calls it success. §15.

         0.4.0 SHIPPED NO BINARY, and the number is spent rather than reused. Its
         tag was pushed while the core it depends on was still being indexed by
         nuget.org, so restore failed; the rerun then hung 10m20s in the test job
         on the very defect above. §15.1, and §12.3 for the last version this
         happened to. -->
    <Version>0.4.1</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Db.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Accounts.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ServerIdentity.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Mailbox.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Presence.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Server.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The wire types, the codec, the sealed envelope, the framing and the
         identity primitives, shared with the desktop application so there is
         one implementation of the protocol rather than two kept in step by
         hand. Its own only dependency is FSharp.Core.

         0.2.2 rather than 0.2.0, and the difference is not the protocol — it is
         the same wire, frame for frame. 0.2.0 and 0.2.1 left FSharp.Core
         implicit, so each declared whatever floor the machine that packed it
         happened to ship, and restoring either here produced an NU1605
         downgrade against a package this project had just been told to trust.
         That warning, raised from this file, is how the defect was found; both
         versions are unlisted on nuget.org now. Pegasus_Design.md §7.1 in the
         EmuSen.Pegasus repository carries the full account.

         0.3.0 is not the protocol either — still the same wire, frame for
         frame, and Hello still says 4. It is the relicence: 0.2.x is
         GPL-3.0-or-later and 0.3.0 is MIT. This bump is load-bearing rather
         than housekeeping, because §11 relicenses THIS repository to MIT and a
         program cannot honestly claim MIT while linking a GPL library. The
         version below is what makes the LICENSE file beside it true.

         0.4.0 IS the protocol, for the first time since 0.2.0: Hello now says 5,
         and a 0.3.0 client cannot sign in to a server built against this. The
         envelope changed shape — a routed frame names its channel and a delivery
         names the post it came out of — and the envelope is precisely the part
         this server reads WITHOUT holding a key, so this is not a version a
         relay could shrug at. §13 carries what the channel bought and why the
         mailbox had to change with it.

         THIS REPOSITORY CANNOT BUILD UNTIL 0.4.0 IS ON NUGET.ORG, and that is
         the ordinary consequence of a protocol written across two repositories
         at once rather than a defect. The core is published from a `core-v*`
         tag in the EmuSen.Pegasus repository and this restores it from
         nuget.org like any other package. To work on both at the same time
         before it is published, add a `local-packages/` folder feed to
         NuGet.config and fill it with `dotnet pack src/EmuSen.Pegasus.Core -c
         Release -o <chariot>/local-packages` — and read the note in that file
         about the cache first, because a rebuilt 0.4.0 is ignored in favour of
         the copy already extracted under ~/.nuget/packages. -->
    <PackageReference Include="EmuSen.Pegasus.Core" Version="0.4.0" />
    <!-- Microsoft.Data.Sqlite alone resolves SQLitePCLRaw 2.1.11, which carries
         a high-severity advisory (GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q) - the very advisory §2.2
         cites against the stack this replaces. The 3.0.5 bundle pin overrides
         it. Keep the pair together. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.10" />
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="3.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
